│                         User Browser                        │
│                     (localhost:3000)                        │
└─────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────┘
                              │ HTTP/HTTPS
                              ▼
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                    WeatherApp (Web Server)                  │
│                   Node.js / Python Flask                    │
└─────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────┘
                              │ API Calls
                              ▼
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                   OpenWeatherMap API                        │
│                   (External Service)                        │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
2.2 Product Functions
•	Location Search: Users can search for weather by city name or ZIP code
•	Current Weather Display: Shows temperature, humidity, wind speed, and conditions
•	5-Day Forecast: Displays daily weather predictions with icons
•	Weather Alerts: Shows severe weather warnings for the searched location
•	Unit Toggle: Allows switching between Celsius and Fahrenheit
•	Responsive Layout: Adapts to desktop, tablet, and mobile screen sizes
2.3 User Classes and Characteristics
User Class	Description	Technical Expertise	Frequency
General User	End user checking weather	Low	High
Demonstrator	Student presenting to instructor	Medium	Single use
Evaluator	Instructor assessing functionality	High	Single use
2.4 Operating Environment
•	Server Platform: Local development machine running Windows, macOS, or Linux
•	Web Browser: Modern browsers (Chrome 90+, Firefox 88+, Safari 14+, Edge 90+)
•	Network: Internet connection required for API calls
•	Development Environment: Node.js 16+ or Python 3.8+ with Flask
2.5 Design and Implementation Constraints
•	Must use a public weather API (OpenWeatherMap recommended)
•	Must run entirely on localhost without external hosting
•	Must be implemented using HTML, CSS, and JavaScript (no paid frameworks required)
•	API key must be stored in environment variables, not hardcoded
2.6 User Documentation
•	README.md: Installation and setup instructions
•	Inline Code Comments: Explanations of key functions
•	User Guide: One-page PDF describing how to use the application
2.7 Assumptions and Dependencies
•	Assumptions:
o	User has internet connectivity for API calls
o	User has a modern web browser installed
o	The OpenWeatherMap API remains available and free tier sufficient
•	Dependencies:
o	OpenWeatherMap API key must be obtained by developer
o	Local development environment must support chosen technology stack
________________________________________
3. External Interface Requirements
3.1 User Interfaces
•	Search Bar: Text input with search button, auto-complete not required
•	Current Weather Card: Displays location name, temperature, condition icon, humidity, wind speed
•	Forecast Section: Horizontal scroll or grid of 5 daily forecast cards
•	Alerts Section: Yellow/red banner displayed when alerts exist
•	Unit Toggle: Button to switch °C/°F with immediate UI update
•	Error Messages: User-friendly messages for invalid locations or API failures
3.2 Hardware Interfaces
No direct hardware interfaces. Application runs on standard consumer computing devices.
3.3 Software Interfaces
Interface	Purpose	Protocol
OpenWeatherMap API	Retrieve current weather and forecast data	HTTPS REST
Local Web Server	Serve application files	HTTP on localhost
3.4 Communications Interfaces
•	HTTP/HTTPS: All communication between client, server, and external API
•	API Format: JSON payloads
•	Rate Limiting: Respect free tier limits (60 calls/minute for OpenWeatherMap)
