Tabular Specification: View Weather Alerts
Element	Description
Use Case Name	View Weather Alerts
Actors	General User
Description	User sees severe weather warnings for the searched location
Preconditions	User has searched for a valid location; API supports alerts
Postconditions	Alert banner displayed if alerts exist; hidden if none
Main Flow	1. System checks API response for alert data
2. If alerts exist, system creates alert banner
3. System displays alert severity (warning/watch/advisory)
4. System displays alert description
5. User acknowledges alert by viewing
Alternative Flows	A1 - Multiple Alerts: System stacks alerts or shows count
Exceptions	E1 - API Does Not Provide Alerts: Alert section remains hidden
Priority	Medium
Business Rules	Alerts displayed prominently at top; Red for warnings, orange for watches, yellow for advisories
________________________________________
4.2 Functional Requirements (Minimum 10)
ID	Requirement Description	Priority
REQ-1	The system shall allow users to search for weather by entering a city name.	High
REQ-2	The system shall allow users to search for weather by entering a 5-digit ZIP code.	Medium
REQ-3	The system shall display current temperature for the searched location.	High
REQ-4	The system shall display current weather conditions (e.g., sunny, rainy, cloudy) with an icon.	High
REQ-5	The system shall display current humidity percentage.	High
REQ-6	The system shall display current wind speed.	Medium
REQ-7	The system shall display a 5-day weather forecast with daily high/low temperatures.	High
REQ-8	The system shall display weather alerts (warnings, watches) when applicable.	Medium
REQ-9	The system shall provide a button to toggle temperature display between Celsius and Fahrenheit.	Medium
REQ-10	The system shall validate user input and display an error message for invalid locations.	High
REQ-11	The system shall handle API errors gracefully with user-friendly messages.	High
REQ-12	The system shall display the last updated timestamp for weather data.	Low
________________________________________
4.3 Non-Functional Requirements (Minimum 5 with Taxonomy)
Product Requirements
Usability Requirements
ID	Requirement Description	Category
NFR-1	The user interface shall load and display weather data within 3 seconds of search submission.	Efficiency (Performance)
NFR-2	The application shall be responsive and function properly on screen widths from 320px to 1920px.	Usability
NFR-3	The application shall display clear error messages (e.g., "City not found") rather than technical errors.	Usability
Dependability Requirements
ID	Requirement Description	Category
NFR-4	The system shall handle API timeout failures by displaying a message and allowing retry without crashing.	Dependability (Robustness)
Security Requirements
ID	Requirement Description	Category
NFR-5	The API key shall be stored in environment variables, never exposed in client-side code.	Security
Organizational Requirements
Environmental Requirements
ID	Requirement Description	Category
NFR-6	The application shall run on Windows, macOS, and Linux development environments without modification.	Organizational (Environmental)
Operational Requirements
ID	Requirement Description	Category
NFR-7	The application shall start with a single command (e.g., npm start or python app.py) from the command line.	Organizational (Operational)
External Requirements
Legislative/Security Requirements
ID	Requirement Description	Category
NFR-8	The application shall not store or log user search history or any personal data.	External (Legislative/Privacy)
________________________________________
5. Other Requirements
5.1 Performance Requirements
•	API calls must complete within 5 seconds or timeout with error message
•	UI must remain interactive during API calls (non-blocking)
•	Application bundle size must be under 2 MB
5.2 Safety Requirements
No safety-critical requirements for this demonstration application.
5.3 Security Requirements
•	API key must not be committed to version control
•	No user authentication required
5.4 Software Quality Attributes
•	Maintainability: Code must be commented and follow consistent naming conventions
•	Testability: Core functions (unit conversion, API calls) must be testable independently
•	Portability: Application must run on any machine with Node.js or Python installed
5.5 Business Rules
•	Free API tier limited to 60 calls per minute
•	Search requires minimum 2 characters for city, exactly 5 digits for ZIP
•	Forecast displays days 1-5 only (not including current day)
________________________________________
6. Requirements Elicitation (2 Methods)
Method 1: Interview with AI-Stakeholder (Zero-Shot Prompting)
(Used Chat GPT)
Prompt Used:
"You are a stakeholder for a weather application that will be demonstrated in a university software development class. Please answer the following questions as a user who checks weather daily:
1. What features would you expect to see when you open the app?
2. What frustrates you about existing weather apps?
3. What information is most important to you on the main screen?
4. How do you prefer to search for locations?
5. What would make you trust the weather data shown?"
AI Response (Stakeholder Role):
1.	"I expect to see the current temperature immediately without clicking anything. A 5-day forecast is essential for planning my week."
2.	"I hate when apps show me ads or ask for my location when I just want to type in a city. Also, when the app doesn't tell me when the data was last updated, I don't know if it's current."
3.	"Main screen needs: current temp, feels-like temp, condition (sunny/rainy), chance of rain, and wind speed. Humidity is helpful too."
4.	"I prefer typing city names. ZIP code search is fine too, but city name is most natural. I don't want to use GPS."
5.	"Seeing the source of the data (like 'Data from OpenWeatherMap') and a timestamp gives me confidence. If there's an alert, I want it highlighted clearly."
Requirements Derived:
•	REQ-3 (display current temperature)
•	REQ-4 (display weather condition)
•	REQ-12 (last updated timestamp)
•	REQ-10 (validate user input - location-based, not GPS)
•	Requirement to display data source (added to UI requirements)
________________________________________
Method 2: Interview with AI-Stakeholder (Chain-of-Thought Prompting)
Prompt Used:
"You are a software engineering instructor evaluating student projects. Think through this step-by-step:
Step 1: Consider what makes a weather app suitable for academic demonstration.
*Step 2: Think about common mistakes students make when building API-integrated apps.*
Step 3: Consider how to evaluate if the app meets learning objectives.
Step 4: Now, provide 5 key requirements this weather app must have to receive full credit.
Show your reasoning for each step before listing the requirements."
AI Response:
Step 1 Reasoning: Academic demos need to show clear functionality without relying on complex infrastructure. The app must run reliably on the instructor's machine (localhost) with minimal setup.
Step 2 Reasoning: Students often hardcode API keys, which then get exposed. They also fail to handle errors (like invalid city names) causing the app to crash or hang.
Step 3 Reasoning: Evaluation focuses on requirement completeness, proper use of APIs, clean UI, and error handling. Code quality matters but for requirements, we need verifiable features.
Step 4 - 5 Requirements for Full Credit:
1.	Application must start and run without configuration beyond API key setup (single command).
2.	All API calls must be non-blocking with loading indicators.
3.	Invalid search input must trigger user-friendly error messages, not console errors.
4.	API key must be externalized (environment variable) and not visible in browser dev tools network tab.
5.	The 5-day forecast must be clearly distinct from current weather, with proper date labels.
Requirements Derived:
•	NFR-7 (single command startup)
•	REQ-11 (graceful API error handling)
•	NFR-5 (API key security)
•	Requirement for loading indicators (added to UI section)
•	REQ-7 with requirement for proper date labels (added to functional spec)
